omial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ about $x=0$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-81676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!} \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=-9 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =27\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(0) & =27\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =1+0\\cdot x-9\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-81676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a963d74ef73dcee608e6f282405dde1471a8fbdbf8fa7937a404f9d0a849ad9b"} {"question" "q-81676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-81676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "1-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "x+\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2}"] ["math" "x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-81676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ about $x=-1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(-1),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(-1)=-{e}^{-(-1)}=-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-1)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-e)=-\\frac{e}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $-\\frac{e}{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a963d74ef73dcee608e6f282405dde1471a8fbdbf8fa7937a404f9d0a849ad9b"} {"question" "q-351875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{e}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-15374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ about $x=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-15374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(0).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x-2)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(0),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(0)=-\\frac{6}{(0-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{6}{16}=-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8})=-\\frac{3}{8\\cdot 6}=-\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{1}{16}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-15374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a963d74ef73dcee608e6f282405dde1471a8fbdbf8fa7937a404f9d0a849ad9b"} {"question" "q-15374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-15374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{16}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{16}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{24}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-15374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{16}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+x)$ about $x=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(0).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3+x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(3+x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(3+x)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(0),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{2}{(3+0)^{3}}=\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot \\frac{2}{27}=\\frac{2}{6\\cdot 27}=\\frac{1}{81}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{81}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a963d74ef73dcee608e6f282405dde1471a8fbdbf8fa7937a404f9d0a849ad9b"} {"question" "q-351863" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351863" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{81}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ about $x=-1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =8{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(-1),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(-1)=8{e}^{2\\cdot (-1)}=8{e}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-1)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 8{e}^{-2}=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a963d74ef73dcee608e6f282405dde1471a8fbdbf8fa7937a404f9d0a849ad9b"} {"question" "q-351873" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-351873" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3e^2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-125925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-3x}$ about $x=-\\frac{1}{3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-125925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(-\\frac{1}{3}).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-27{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(-\\frac{1}{3}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(-\\frac{1}{3})=-27\\cdot {e}^{-3\\cdot (-1/3)}=-27e.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-\\frac{1}{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-27e)=-\\frac{9e}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{9e}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-125925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a963d74ef73dcee608e6f282405dde1471a8fbdbf8fa7937a404f9d0a849ad9b"} {"question" "q-125925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-125925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3e}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2e}"] ["math" "-\\frac{9e}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2e}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "q-125925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{9e}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d96190022f56e4e82b7ae8099c5020d953b66af5f509580b4f40574df736bd7"} {"question" "e-7115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)$ be a function with $f(-1)=-1, f'(-1)=3, f''(-1)=-6$ and $f'''(-1)=6.$ What is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/example-7115.json" "file_sha256" "c2114e2b684c903a1c6f570c7987973a47ccdcd9d354323e1bcb56a947e84015"} {"question" "e-7115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(-1)}{3!}(x+1)^{3} \\\\  & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(-1)}{6}(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =-6 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =-1+3(x+1)+\\frac{(-6)}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{6}{6}(x+1)^{3} \\\\  & =-1+3(x+1)-3(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/example-7115.json" "file_sha256" "c2114e2b684c903a1c6f570c7987973a47ccdcd9d354323e1bcb56a947e84015"} {"question" "e-741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = \\ln(1+x)$ about $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/example-741.json" "file_sha256" "e6161aad25e927abf4c2ab55467d7e8504715854e10357c8ba16f038fd32823a"} {"question" "e-741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_3(x) = f(0) + f'(0)(x-0) + \\dfrac{1}{2!}f''(0)(x-0)^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!}f'''(0)(x-0)^3.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0),$ $f'(0),$ $f''(0),$ and $f'''(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+0)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =(1+x)^{-1} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =(1+0)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-(1+x)^{-2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =-(1+0)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =2(1+x)^{-3} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(0) & =2(1+0)^{-3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(0),$ $f'(0),$ $f''(0),$ and $f'''(0)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =0+(1)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-1)(x-0)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}(2)(x-0)^{3} \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/example-741.json" "file_sha256" "e6161aad25e927abf4c2ab55467d7e8504715854e10357c8ba16f038fd32823a"} {"question" "e-742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = e^{-2x}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/example-742.json" "file_sha256" "5e8f9ba2791c1fa9088b8ac201eb7c49e6986a3c082f5adead76b341d0581e7b"} {"question" "e-742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ is $\\dfrac{1}{3!}f^{(3)}(1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-8{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3!}f^{(3)}(1) =\\dfrac{1}{6} \\left( -8e^{-2} \\right) = -\\dfrac{4}{3e^2}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ is $-\\dfrac{4}{3e^2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/example-742.json" "file_sha256" "5e8f9ba2791c1fa9088b8ac201eb7c49e6986a3c082f5adead76b341d0581e7b"}] "usage" {"q-9360" [] "e-742" [] "q-51235" [] "e-741" [] "e-7115" []} "basis" 1304 "retractions" [[17592186101384 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be a function with $f(4) = 1, f^{′}(4) =-1, f^{″}(4) =-2$ and $f^{‴}(4) = 12$. Which of the following is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x = 4$?"] [17592186101481 :question/problem "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = \\sin x$ about $x = 0$ is"] [17592186100680 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be a function with $f(-1)=-1, f'(-1)=3, f''(-1)=-6$ and $f'''(-1)=6$. What is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$?"] [17592186100680 :question/worked-solution "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{3}(x) &= f(-1) + f^{′}(-1)(x + 1) + (f^{″}(-1))/(2!)(x + 1)^{2} + (f^{‴}(-1))/(3!)(x + 1)^{3} \\\\\n&= f(-1) + f^{′}(-1)(x + 1) + (f^{″}(-1))/(2)(x + 1)^{2} + (f^{‴}(-1))/(6)(x + 1)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= -1 \\\\\nf^{′}(-1) &= 3 \\\\\nf^{″}(-1) &= -6 \\\\\nf^{‴}(-1) &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{3}(x) &= -1 + 3(x + 1) + ((-6))/(2)(x + 1)^{2} + \\frac{6}{6}(x + 1)^{3} \\\\\n&=-1 + 3(x + 1) - 3(x + 1)^{2} + (x + 1)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100691 :question/problem "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = \\ln(1+x)$ about $x=0$."] [17592186100691 :question/worked-solution "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0$, we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_3(x) = f(0) + f'(0)(x-0) + \\dfrac{1}{2!}f''(0)(x-0)^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!}f'''(0)(x-0)^3\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0)$, $f'(0)$, $f''(0)$, and $f'''(0)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) &= \\ln (1 + x) & ⟹ & f(0)|= \\ln (1 + 0) = 0 \\\\ f^{′}(x)|= (1 + x)^{-1} & ⟹ & f^{′}(0)|= (1 + 0)^{-1} = 1 \\\\ f^{″}(x)|= - (1 + x)^{-2} & ⟹ & f^{″}(0)|= - (1 + 0)^{-2} =-1 \\\\ f^{(3)}(x)|= 2(1 + x)^{-3} & ⟹ & f^{‴}(0)|= 2(1 + 0)^{-3} = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(0)$, $f'(0)$, $f''(0)$, and $f'''(0)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{3}(x) &= 0 + (1)(x - 0) + \\frac{1}{2!}(-1)(x - 0)^{2} + \\frac{1}{3!}(2)(x - 0)^{3} \\\\\n&= x - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{x^{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100692 :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = e^{-2x}$?"] [17592186100692 :question/worked-solution "The coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3!}f^{(3)}(1)\n$$\n\nLet's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= e^{-2x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= -2e^{-2x} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 4e^{-2x} \\\\\nf^{(3)}(x) &= -8e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3!}f^{(3)}(1) =\\dfrac{1}{6} \\left(-8e^{-2} \\right) = -\\dfrac{4}{3e^2}\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{4}{3e^2}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186099901 17592186099902 17592186099903 17592186099904 17592186099905 17592186100483 17592186100484 17592186100485 17592186100486 17592186100487] "immutable_fields" [[17592186099900 :answer-field/correct] [17592186099900 :answer-field/id] [17592186099900 :answer-field/key] [17592186099900 :answer-field/type] [17592186100482 :answer-field/correct] [17592186100482 :answer-field/id] [17592186100482 :answer-field/key] [17592186100482 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
